  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warming trend will bring above average high temperatures in the \nmid to upper 80s by late week, along with dry conditions.\n\nThe Nor'easter continues to weaken today with just a weak sfc \nreflection and limited moisture as it slowly traverses the nearshore \nwaters off of the northern New England coast. High pressure builds \nover eastern CONUS from the Great Lake region the first part of the \nweek, allowing dry conditions and a rather substantial warming trend \nbeginning today and continuing through the end of the week. Highs \ntoday will range from the upper 60s/around 70F on the Eastern Shore \nto the mid to upper 70s for mainland VA and NC. Tuesday will be a \nfew degrees warmer, flirting with the 80s for southern portions of \nthe area. Widespread 80s Wednesday and potentially near 90F Thursday \nand Friday are likely with the Eastern Shore seeing temps a few \ndegrees cooler each day. The sfc and upper-air synoptics support dry \nwx through most of the work week. By late Friday into the weekend, \nsome sort of southern stream or elongated frontal system could bring \nprecip chances back to the region. Models continue to disagree on \ntiming and evolution of any potential system; thus, NBM PoPs are \nonly 20-50% Friday through the weekend. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Coastal flooding impacts continue to lessen with\neasing winds. 1-2 additional rounds of minor flooding remain \npossible through this evening, primarily on the Eastern Shore.\n\nCoastal flood impacts continue to wind down across the local area\nthis morning. Coastal Flood headlines on the tidal Rappahannock and\nPotomac sites were allowed to expire on time earlier this \nmorning. It will take time for water to move out of the upper \nChesapeake Bay, and accordingly a few locations on the Lower \nMaryland Eastern Shore will need another round of Advisories \nthrough this afternoon's high tide cycle to cover residual minor\ntidal flooding in the middle Chesapeake Bay. Beyond that \ntimeframe, while tides will remain elevated through midweek due \nto higher astronomical tides in association with the lunar \ncycle, only nuisance to near-minor tidal levels are anticipated \nthrough midweek.",
